  "preamble": "The shift from the voiceless dental fricative [θ] to the voiceless stop [t] is known as stopping. It is a very common dialectal variation, particularly in languages that lack dental fricatives in their core phonology. In Semitic, Aramaic (talgā 'snow', tawrā 'ox'), the Arabic vernaculars of Egypt, the Levant and Malta (talāta, tlieta 'three') and Soqotri among the Modern South Arabian languages all replaced Proto-Semitic *θ with t.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Stopping",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Aramaic",
      "languageFamily": "Northwest Semitic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Semitic *ṯalg- 'snow'",
          "to": "Jewish Aramaic תַּלְגָּא talgā, Syriac ܬܱ݁ܠܓܳܐ talgā 'snow'",
          "note": "Contrast Hebrew šéleḡ and Arabic ṯalj."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Semitic *ṯawr- 'bull'",
          "to": "תַּוְרָא tawrā 'ox'"
        },
